WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.567
A real processor runs instructions

2
00:00:02.633 --> 00:00:03.500
out of order.

3
00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:06.687
The task gives the predictor two callbacks

4
00:00:06.687 --> 00:00:07.676
decode notify

5
00:00:07.676 --> 00:00:10.249
when an instruction claims a register

6
00:00:10.249 --> 00:00:11.569
and execute notify

7
00:00:11.569 --> 00:00:14.010
when it runs and the value is known.

8
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.120
Follow register three.

9
00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.430
Instructions ten and twelve both claim it.

10
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.300
Twelve runs first and writes five

11
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.820
so prediction thirteen uses five.

12
00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.890
Then ten runs late and writes nine.

13
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.787
That value is stale

14
00:00:29.787 --> 00:00:32.880
so prediction fourteen still uses five.

15
00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:35.510
Once fifteen claims the register

16
00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.790
nothing is usable until it runs.

17
00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.780
Only the newest claimant's value counts.

18
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.936
The task keeps TAGE

19
00:00:42.936 --> 00:00:45.063
the rest of the corrector

20
00:00:45.063 --> 00:00:46.560
and the simulator.

21
00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.367
It removes the register-value component

22
00:00:49.429 --> 00:00:51.763
and the agent writes it in nine marked

23
00:00:51.824 --> 00:00:53.790
regions of one C plus plus file.

24
00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.637
Which registers to use

25
00:00:55.637 --> 00:00:57.458
and how big the tables are

26
00:00:57.458 --> 00:00:59.020
are the agent's choice.

27
00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.686
The whole predictor must fit a hundred

28
00:01:01.747 --> 00:01:03.271
and ninety-two kibibytes

29
00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:05.770
and the reference uses almost every bit.

30
00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.462
Each submission runs a simulator over the

31
00:01:09.538 --> 00:01:12.270
hundred and five traces of CBP 2025.

32
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.789
The score combines mispredictions

33
00:01:14.854 --> 00:01:16.300
and wrong-path cycles.

34
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.677
The paper's own averages give the

35
00:01:18.738 --> 00:01:20.703
reference thirty-one point five

36
00:01:20.764 --> 00:01:23.405
and five percent fewer of both errors would

37
00:01:23.466 --> 00:01:25.370
give only thirty-two point six.

38
00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.110
The pass rule is much harder.

39
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.287
A predictor must make strictly

40
00:01:30.348 --> 00:01:31.627
fewer mispredictions

41
00:01:31.627 --> 00:01:33.941
and strictly fewer wrong-path cycles

42
00:01:33.941 --> 00:01:35.159
than the reference

43
00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:37.900
on every one of the hundred and five traces.

44
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.330
A tie anywhere fails.

45
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.879
And the task warns that noise alone moves a trace's

46
00:01:43.942 --> 00:01:46.200
mispredictions by about one percent.

47
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.380
Pause and predict.

48
00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:50.543
A new predictor is two percent

49
00:01:50.604 --> 00:01:51.692
better on average

50
00:01:51.692 --> 00:01:54.170
with one percent of noise on each trace.

51
00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:56.249
What is its chance of winning

52
00:01:56.308 --> 00:01:57.560
all hundred and five?

53
00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.670
In our model, about nine percent.

54
00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.300
At one percent better, it is almost zero.

55
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.434
It takes about three percent

56
00:02:09.497 --> 00:02:11.090
better to be fairly safe.

57
00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:13.281
No model passed

58
00:02:13.281 --> 00:02:15.850
and none scored above the reference.

59
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.167
Claude Opus 5.5 came closest

60
00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:19.998
at thirty-one point three

61
00:02:20.066 --> 00:02:22.915
and all sixteen runs scored between thirty

62
00:02:22.983 --> 00:02:25.560
point nine and thirty-one point three.

63
00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:27.993
Opus tracked the newest producer

64
00:02:28.058 --> 00:02:29.750
of thirty-three registers.

65
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.302
At each prediction

66
00:02:31.365 --> 00:02:33.262
it took the six registers with

67
00:02:33.326 --> 00:02:34.780
the most recent values

68
00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:36.678
and looked each one up twice

69
00:02:36.678 --> 00:02:39.334
by its exact value and by a coarse class

70
00:02:39.334 --> 00:02:42.180
so that nearby values share what they learn.

71
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:43.005
So

72
00:02:43.005 --> 00:02:46.520
use a value only from its newest producer.

73
00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.082
A pass needs a win on every trace

74
00:02:49.082 --> 00:02:51.750
with about one percent of noise on each.

75
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.429
And rebuilding the paper is not enough

76
00:02:54.489 --> 00:02:56.221
the reference seems to be the

77
00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:57.594
paper's own component

78
00:02:57.654 --> 00:02:59.445
and a good rebuild lands about

79
00:02:59.505 --> 00:03:00.580
one percent worse.

80
00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.450
Next, KAMI.
